{"id":"phantastes-en_sct10_f4","schemaVersion":1,"fragmentId":"sha1:6335a9fa321d8053b7472fc85d45cff4b278ecfc","generationHash":"sha1:2ce838f72dd8f7e4a44313aa3b8aeacb4b620d53","title":"Phantastes - IV (Fragment 4)","text":"“Why do you call yourself a beech-tree?” I said.\n\n“Because I am one,” she replied, in the same low, musical, murmuring voice.\n\n“You are a woman,” I returned.\n\n“Do you think so? Am I very like a woman then?”\n\n“You are a very beautiful woman. Is it possible you should not know it?”\n\n“I am very glad you think so. I fancy I feel like a woman sometimes. I do so tonight⁠—and always when the rain drips from my hair. For there is an old prophecy in our woods that one day we shall all be men and women like you. Do you know anything about it in your region? Shall I be very happy when I am a woman? I fear not; for it is always in nights like these that I feel like one. But I long to be a woman for all that.”\n\nI had let her talk on, for her voice was like a solution of all musical sounds. I now told her that I could hardly say whether women were happy or not. I knew one who had not been happy; and for my part, I had often longed for Fairy Land, as she now longed for the world of men. But then neither of us had lived long, and perhaps people grew happier as they grew older. Only I doubted it. I could not help sighing. She felt the sigh, for her arms were still round me. She asked me how old I was.\n\n“Twenty-one,” said I.\n\n“Why, you baby!” said she, and kissed me with the sweetest kiss of winds and odors. There was a cool faithfulness in the kiss that revived my heart wonderfully. I felt that I feared the dreadful Ash no more.\n\n“What did the horrible Ash want with me?” I said.\n\n“I am not quite sure, but I think he wants to bury you at the foot of his tree. But he shall not touch you, my child.”\n\n“Are all the ash-trees as dreadful as he?”\n\n“Oh, no. They are all disagreeable selfish creatures⁠—(what horrid men they will make, if it be true!)⁠—but this one has a hole in his heart that nobody knows of but one or two; and he is always trying to fill it up, but he cannot. That must be what he wanted you for. I wonder if he will ever be a man. If he is, I hope they will kill him.”","meta":{"bookId":"phantastes-en","bookTitle":"Phantastes","bookLanguage":"en","author":"George MacDonald","seriesId":"george-macdonald","seriesTitle":"George MacDonald","sectionIndex":10,"sectionTitle":"IV","fragmentIndex":4,"fragmentsInSection":5,"sourceFile":"text/chapter-4.xhtml","stats":{"wordCount":408,"uniqueWordCount":220,"paragraphCount":13,"vocabularyCount":96,"levelDistribution":{"A1":32,"A2":17,"B1":24,"B2":18,"C1":2,"C2":3}}},"vocabulary":[{"word":"arms","lemma":"arm","lemmaId":"arm","pos":"noun","source":"word","wordKey":"arm_noun","level":"A1","translations":{"ru":{"translation":"руки"},"uk":{"translation":"руку"}},"_spacyLemma":"arm","exampleStart":1146,"exampleEnd":1154,"matchedForm":"arms","levelSource":"vocabulary_index","ipa":"ɑːm","difficultyScore":0.508571,"difficultySource":"zipf"},{"word":"ash","lemma":"ash","lemmaId":"ash","pos":"noun","source":"word","wordKey":"ash_noun","level":"B2","translations":{"ru":{"translation":"пепел"},"uk":{"translation":"попелу"}},"_spacyLemma":"ash","exampleStart":1410,"exampleEnd":1426,"matchedForm":"Ash","levelSource":"vocabulary_index","ipa":"ˈæʃɪz","difficultyScore":0.671429,"difficultySource":"zipf"},{"word":"ash-trees","lemma":"ash tree","lemmaId":"ash_tree","pos":"phrase","type":"phrase","source":"spacy_phrase","wordKey":"ash_tree_phrase","level":"B1","translations":{"ru":{"translation":"Песчаное дерево"},"uk":{"translation":"попіл"}},"exampleStart":1605,"exampleEnd":1649,"matchedForm":"ash-trees","phraseMatch":{"text":"ash-trees","startInExample":16,"endInExample":25},"levelSource":"head_verb_index"},{"word":"asked","lemma":"ask","lemmaId":"ask","pos":"verb","source":"word","wordKey":"ask_verb","level":"A1","translations":{"ru":{"translation":"спросил"},"uk":{"translat